const faqs = [
  { q: "Is it easy to find vegetarian food in Lhasa?", a: "It is easier than most travelers expect, especially near Barkhor Street. Many Tibetan Buddhist monasteries serve vegetarian meals, and Lhasa has a growing number of dedicated vegetarian restaurants. The challenge is clarifying what vegetarian means in context: some restaurants use yak broth in vegetable dishes without considering it meat." },
  { q: "Where can I find halal food in Lhasa?", a: "Lhasa has a significant Muslim Hui community concentrated near the Lhasa Great Mosque east of Barkhor Street. Several halal restaurants serve Hui-style Chinese food: hand-pulled noodles, lamb skewers, flatbreads, and stir-fried dishes. Look for green signage with Arabic script or the halal certification logo." },
  { q: "Is there a mosque in Lhasa?", a: "Yes. The Lhasa Great Mosque (Lasa Qingzhen Dasi) is located east of Barkhor Street. It was built in 1716 and serves the Hui Muslim community. The area around the mosque is the best place to find halal food. Non-Muslims are generally welcome to visit the mosque compound but not the prayer hall during prayer times." },
  { q: "Can I find vegan food in Lhasa?", a: "Vegan options are more limited than vegetarian. Tibetan monasteries typically serve lacto-vegetarian meals that include dairy (yak butter, yogurt). Some dedicated vegetarian restaurants can prepare vegan dishes on request. Specify no dairy, no butter, no eggs clearly. The phrase I don't eat any animal products in Chinese: wǒ bù chī rènhé dòngwù chǎnpǐn." },
  { q: "What vegetarian Tibetan dishes can I order?", a: "Shogo momo (potato-cheese dumplings), tsampa with sugar and yak butter, sweet tea, yak yogurt, Tibetan bread, sha phaley (substitute vegetable filling), thenthuk (request vegetable broth), and simple rice with stir-fried seasonal vegetables. Always confirm no meat broth in soups and stews." },
];
